# Every nonnegative q-coefficient of j is at least 1

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.one_le_coeff_jq` (`1a092aee-6c1c-510a-a2b0-6a3ed4219d58`), by
Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `c7a99add-fe2c-5381-9052-63b26fc74c52`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $n$ be a natural number. The assertion is that the coefficient of the $q$-expansion `jq` at the
integer index $(n : \mathbb{Z})$ is at least $1$ as a rational number, i.e. $1 \le
\mathrm{coeff}_{n}(\mathtt{jq})$. Here `jq` is the formal $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant
as set up in the project, a series with rational coefficients indexed by $\mathbb{Z}$, whose
coefficients at the indices $0, 1, 2, \dots$ are the classical values $744, 196884, 21493760,
\dots$; the statement ranges only over nonnegative indices, so it says nothing about the coefficient
at $-1$ (the leading $q^{-1}$ term) or at other negative indices, and it records only the inequality
$\ge 1$ rather than integrality or the exact values. In particular every coefficient of `jq` in
nonnegative degree is nonzero and positive.

This positivity bound replaces the classical fact that the $q$-expansion $j(q) = q^{-1} + 744 +
196884q + \cdots$ has positive integral coefficients, in the weak form needed downstream. It is used
to separate $j(q^{\ell})$ from the subfield generated by $j(q)$, and thence in the irreducibility
and splitting statements for the modular polynomial $\Phi_\ell$
([`ModularCurve.PhiGen.evalSymm_of_splits`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.evalSymm_of_splits),
[`ModularCurve.PhiGen.phiIrreducible_of_splits`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.phiIrreducible_of_splits),
[`ModularCurve.jqNModC_prime_not_mem_adjoin_of_charZero`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.jqNModC_prime_not_mem_adjoin_of_charZero)).

